    <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-300">Body Region</span>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mt-2">Shoulder</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-2">Shoulder acupressure targets the muscles that hold tension between the neck and upper arm. GB21 (Jianjing) on top of the shoulder and LI15 at the joint are the go-to points for shoulder pain, stiffness, and frozen-shoulder patterns.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mb-10 flex justify-center"><svg viewBox="0 0 140 100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-40 h-28"><path d="M10 50 Q30 20 70 30 Q110 20 130 50" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"></path><circle cx="30" cy="40" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="70" cy="35" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="110" cy="40" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="1.5" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.4"></circle><circle cx="90" cy="50" r="1.5" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.4"></circle><text x="70" y="85" font-family="system-ui" font-size="10" fill="#2d6a4f" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Shoulder</text></svg></div>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Do Shoulder Acupressure Points Matter?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The shoulders connect the neck, upper back, and arms, and they absorb more daily strain than most people realize. Lifting, typing, driving, and carrying bags all load the trapezius and deltoid, and stress tends to settle in the same region. Over time this shows up as tightness, aching, limited reach, and the stiffness of a frozen-shoulder pattern. Shoulder points appear in nearly every upper-body treatment we describe: the neck pain guide pairs them with the base of the skull, the back pain guide uses the same network for radiating discomfort, and GB21 appears throughout for its broad reach.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">In TCM, the shoulder is crossed by the Large Intestine, Small Intestine, and Gallbladder meridians, and GB21 near the crest of the trapezius is a classic point for moving qì that feels stuck in the upper body. Physically, the shoulder's large surface muscles respond to firm, sustained pressure, which can help tightly contracted fibres relax and supports easier range of motion without forcing it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Are the Key Points in This Region?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-5">Match your shoulder complaint to a point: tension at the top, pain at the joint, or stiffness behind the shoulder.</p>
    <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Points in this region</h3>
    <div class="space-y-3">
