  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How Do You Stimulate Shoulder Points?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Approach the shoulder from behind or the side and press with the pads of your fingers or the heel of your hand. Increase pressure slowly over 10–15 seconds at a 90-degree angle, hold for 30–60 seconds as you feel the muscles soften, then release gradually. For points you cannot easily reach, press the opposite shoulder with the hand of the other side. Breathe evenly throughout. One important exception: GB21 (Jianjing) should not be pressed during pregnancy.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can acupressure help frozen shoulder?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis) is a stiffening of the shoulder joint capsule that typically improves slowly over months or years. Acupressure will not unfreeze the joint by itself, but holding points like LI15 and SI9 may ease the surrounding muscle tension and make gentle range-of-motion exercises more comfortable. Use it as part of a broader plan with stretching and professional care rather than as a self-contained cure. If movement is severely limited or pain keeps you awake, see a clinician.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why is GB21 used so often for tension?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">GB21 (Jianjing) sits at the highest point of the trapezius, the exact spot where upper-body tension concentrates. It is one of the most frequently recommended points for neck and shoulder tightness because it is easy to locate, accessible from behind, and produces a pronounced deep, dull sensation. Because the trapezius connects the neck, shoulder, and upper back, relieving this one point can soften tension across an entire region. Just remember to skip it during pregnancy.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Hold each shoulder point for 30–60 seconds, pressing both sides where the point appears on both shoulders. Deepen the pressure slowly over 10–15 seconds until you feel a deep, dull ache (deqi), and continue breathing evenly. A minute per point, done daily on both sides, is a sensible baseline for a tension-heavy region. Ease off if the muscle feels bruised or unusually tender afterward.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
    <a href="/atlas/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to Body Atlas</a>
  </div>

  <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011", "song-2015"]} note={"General evidence for acupressure from published systematic reviews. See condition-specific pages for targeted studies."} />
